INT. NGUYEN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Dark. A phone flashlight swings across the room and lands on a fridge covered in magnets. A neighbor's dog BARKS outside. Through the window, two teenagers on a trampoline in the next yard use their phones as lanterns, shrieking with laughter.

Kit, 40s, pyjama bottoms and a "World's Okayest Coach" mug, stumbles in and sets down a laptop on the counter. It's at 4 percent. Behind him Jo, 40s, his wife, in a team fleece, carries a stack of whiteboards under one arm and a bag of frozen peas under the other.

The laptop lights up. Slack, glowing, on screen.

ON SCREEN: #coaches-all - Marcus: "Anyone got the lineup for Saturday? lights are out here too lol"

ON SCREEN: #coaches-all - Priya: "Kit?? you're on the sheet"

He winces. Taps a key. The screen dims.

KIT
Battery saver. Battery saver.

JO
You said you'd print it.

KIT
The printer needs power. It's literally in the name.

She slaps the peas onto the counter, sets down the boards, and starts scribbling in the dark with a marker that doesn't work. He holds the phone light over her shoulder while she draws Xs and Os and arrows pointing off the edge of the board. He frowns, she frowns, and the dog outside barks again, and the teenagers on the trampoline shout something about a penalty.

JO
Left wing's a problem.

KIT
Left wing's a Tuesday problem. It's a Saturday problem now.

She turns, glares, and holds out the marker. He takes it. Tries it on the board. Nothing. Shakes it. Tries again. Nothing.

JO
Give it here.

She snatches it back. Bites the cap. Presses hard. A faint grey line appears, then a clean black one, bold as a gunshot.

JO (CONT'D)
There.

KIT
You intimidated a marker.

JO
It knows who's in charge.

The laptop blinks. 2 percent.

ON SCREEN: #coaches-all - Marcus: "ok we're going with 4-4-2 unless someone objects"

ON SCREEN: #coaches-all - Priya: "Kit?? last chance"

Kit lunges. Jo lunges. Their hands collide on the keyboard and the machine makes a tiny, dying chirp.

ON SCREEN: #coaches-all - Kit: "4-3-3 left wing Danny, subs on at 60"

Send. The screen goes black.

Silence. The darkness settles. Even the dog stops.

KIT
Was that right?

JO
No.

He laughs. She laughs. The fridge shudders on, the overhead light blinks to life, and every magnet on the door sings a little as the compressor catches.

They squint at each other in the sudden brightness. He's holding the marker. She's holding the peas.

KIT
Dinner?

JO
Peas.

KIT
Peas.
